workedExamples.solution_text:
Angles on the straight line $DRC$ add to $180^\circ$.

$\begin{aligned}\angle PRQ&=180^\circ-76^\circ-48^\circ\\&=56^\circ\end{aligned}$

$PR=RQ$, so the base angles of $\triangle PRQ$ are equal.

$\begin{aligned}\angle RQP&=(180^\circ-56^\circ)\div2\\&=62^\circ\end{aligned}$

$\angle RCQ=90^\circ$ (an angle of the square).

$\angle RQC=180^\circ-90^\circ-48^\circ=42^\circ$ (angle sum of $\triangle RQC$).

Angles on the straight line $BQC$ add to $180^\circ$.

$\begin{aligned}x&=180^\circ-62^\circ-42^\circ\\&=76^\circ\end{aligned}$

