workedExamples.solution_text:
Draw a line through $C$ parallel to the top and bottom sides of the rectangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (TL) at (-2,1.5);\coordinate (TR) at (2,1.5);\coordinate (BL) at (-2,-1.5);\coordinate (BR) at (2,-1.5);
\coordinate (A) at (-1.5,1.5);\coordinate (B) at (-1.5,-1.5);\coordinate (C) at (1.098,0);\coordinate (L) at (-2,0);
\draw (TL)--(TR)--(BR)--(BL)--cycle;\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;\draw[dashed] (L)--(2,0);
\node[above] at (A) {$A$};\node[below] at (B) {$B$};\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\pic[draw,"$p^\circ$",angle radius=8mm,angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=C--A--TR};
\pic[draw,"$q^\circ$",angle radius=8mm,angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=BR--B--C};
\pic[draw,"$p^\circ$",angle radius=12mm,angle eccentricity=1.3] {angle=A--C--L};
\pic[draw,"$q^\circ$",angle radius=12mm,angle eccentricity=1.3] {angle=L--C--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The upper angle at $C$ equals $p^\circ$ and the lower angle equals $q^\circ$ (alternate angles on parallel lines).

Together they make $\angle ACB=60^\circ$ (an angle of the equilateral triangle).

Therefore $p+q=60$.

