Find $x$, then classify quadrilateral $ABCD$ by testing both pairs of opposite sides. A trapezium has exactly one pair of parallel sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\coordinate (B) at (4,0);\coordinate (C) at (4,3);\coordinate (D) at (0,5.309401077);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\pic[draw,"$90^\circ$",angle radius=6mm,angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=B--A--D};
\pic[draw,"$90^\circ$",angle radius=6mm,angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=C--B--A};
\pic[draw,"$(3x)^\circ$",angle radius=7mm,angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=D--C--B};
\pic[draw,"$(x+20)^\circ$",angle radius=7mm,angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=A--D--C};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The interior angles of a quadrilateral add to $360^\circ$.

$\begin{aligned}90+90+3x+(x+20)&=360\\4x+200&=360\\4x&=160\\x&=40\end{aligned}$

$\angle BCD=3(40)^\circ=120^\circ$ and $\angle CDA=(40+20)^\circ=60^\circ$.

$\angle DAB+\angle ABC=90^\circ+90^\circ=180^\circ$, so $AD\parallel BC$ (converse of the co-interior angle relationship, transversal $AB$).

$\angle BCD+\angle CDA=120^\circ+60^\circ=180^\circ$ tests the same pair, $AD$ and $BC$, using transversal $DC$.

$\angle DAB+\angle CDA=90^\circ+60^\circ=150^\circ$, so $AB$ is not parallel to $DC$ (co-interior angles on transversal $AD$ would add to $180^\circ$).

Exactly one pair of opposite sides is parallel, so $ABCD$ is a trapezium. A parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus or square has two pairs of parallel sides.

